'''''Ruellia strepens''''', commonly known as '''limestone wild petunia''',<ref name="natureserve">{{cite web |title=''Ruellia strepens'' |url=https://explorer.natureserve.org/Taxon/ELEMENT_GLOBAL.2.138843/Ruellia_strepens |website=explorer.natureserve.org |publisher=NatureServe Explorer 2.0 }}</ref> '''limestone ruellia''',<ref name="LBJ"/> '''smooth wild petunia''',<ref name="IL">{{cite web |title=Smooth Wild Petunia (''Ruellia strepens'') |url=https://www.illinoiswildflowers.info/woodland/plants/sm_petunia.htm |website=www.illinoiswildflowers.info }}</ref> or '''wild petunia'''<ref name="MO" >{{cite web |title=''Ruellia strepens'' page |url=http://www.missouriplants.com/Ruellia_strepens_page.html |website=www.missouriplants.com }}</ref> is a species of flowering plant in the family [[Acanthaceae]], native to warmer parts of the central and eastern United States.<ref name="POWO_54614-1" >{{cite POWO |id=54614-1 |title=''Ruellia strepens'' L. |access-date=24 October 2022 }}</ref><ref name="LBJ">{{cite web |url=https://www.wildflower.org/plants/result.php?id_plant=rust2 |title=''Ruellia strepens'' |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=18 June 2015 |website=Plant Database |publisher=Lady Bird Johnson Wildflower Center |access-date=24 October 2022 }}</ref> A [[perennial]] herb, it prefers to grow in moist to mesic, partly shady areas such as streamsides and bottomland forests. In the garden it is hardy to [[Hardiness zone|USDA zone]] 5, and can tolerate nearly full shade.<ref name="MBG_275303" >{{cite web |url=https://www.missouribotanicalgarden.org/PlantFinder/PlantFinderDetails.aspx?taxonid=275303 |title=''Ruellia strepens'' |author=<!--Not stated--> |date= |website=Plant Finder |publisher=Missouri Botanical Garden |access-date=24 October 2022 |quote=...commonly called wild petunia or smooth ruellia }}</ref> 

==Description==
''R. strepena'' grows up to {{cvt|3|ft|m|0|order=flip|}} tall, branching occasionally. The green stems either smooth or sparsely hairy. The leaves are opposite on the stem, have short [[Petiole (botany)|petioles]], and are [[lanceolate]] or [[ovate (botany)|ovate]], tapering to a sharp point at the end. They measure up to {{cvt|5|in|cm|0|order=flip|}} long and {{cvt|3|in|cm|0|order=flip|}} across. Their margins are smooth or slightly undulate.<ref name="IL"/><ref name="MO" />

The flowers are lavender to bluish, growing from nodes near the middle of the stems. They have 5 flaring lobes and are tubular, measuring up to {{cvt|2|in|cm|0|order=flip|}} long.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Denison |first1=Edgar |title=Missouri Wildflowers |date=2017 |publisher=Conservation Commission of the State of Missouri |isbn=978-1-887247-59-7 |pages=98 |edition=Sixth }}</ref><ref name="MBG_275303"/>

Although many herbaceous plants cease growing or even die back after blooming, ''R.{{nbsp}}strepens'' continues to get bigger as its fruits mature, resulting in late season plants that might be twice as tall as they were when flowering.<ref name="MO" />

==Etymology==
The genus name ''Ruellia'' honors [[Jean Ruelle]], a French physician and botanist (1474 – 1537). Its [[specific epithet]] ''strepens'' refers to the rustling noises the plant's seeds make as they uncoil and bury themselves into the ground.<ref>{{cite web |title=SEINet Portal Network - ''Ruellia strepens'' |url=https://swbiodiversity.org/seinet/taxa/index.php?taxon=99374 |website=swbiodiversity.org }}</ref>

==Distribution and habitat==
''R. strepena'' is native in the United States from Nebraska to the west, Texas and Florida to the south, Michigan and Pennsylvania to the north, and the east coast to the east.<ref name="natureserve"/> It is listed as endangered in Michigan<ref>{{cite web |title=Michigan's Rare Plants - Michigan Natural Features Inventory |url=https://mnfi.anr.msu.edu/species/plants |website=mnfi.anr.msu.edu }}</ref> and North Carolina<ref>{{cite web |title=Home {{!}} North Carolina Natural Heritage Data Explorer |url=https://ncnhde.natureserve.org/ |website=ncnhde.natureserve.org }}</ref> and critically imperiled in South Carolina.<ref>{{cite web |title=SC Natural Heritage Program |url=https://schtportal.dnr.sc.gov/portal/apps/sites/#/natural-heritage-program |website=schtportal.dnr.sc.gov }}</ref> It lives in bottomland forests, rich upland forests, river and stream banks, and edges of ponds and lakes. It can also be found occasionally in bottomland prairies and fens, pastures, moist roadsides, and railroads.<ref>{{cite web |title=Wild Petunia (Smooth Ruellia) |url=https://mdc.mo.gov/discover-nature/field-guide/wild-petunia-smooth-ruellia |website=Missouri Department of Conservation |language=en }}</ref>

